# Front-end testing (user-centric)

Each test should answer one question: **did the user-facing interface stay correct?** If the experience is unchanged, the test should stay green after refactors (i18n plumbing, component swaps, Tailwind/class changes, store or hook internals). If the user sees or can do something different, the test should fail. Treat tests as a **contract for the UI**, not a receipt for implementation.

## Queries and assertions

**Prefer** (in roughly this order — see [Testing Library: query priority](https://testing-library.com/docs/queries/about/#priority)):

- Role + accessible name: `getByRole('button', { name: 'Save profile' })`
- Label text: `getByLabelText('Email')`
- Placeholder when that is the only stable user-facing handle: `getByPlaceholderText('…')`

**Avoid** as the primary way to find or assert behavior:

- Translation keys as expected text (or mocks that echo keys instead of real copy)
- Specific React component types or implementation-only props as proof the UI works
- Framework or utility class names (e.g. third-party class prefixes, brittle Tailwind strings)
- Mock call arguments, internal instance/state, or store shape as a substitute for visible outcomes

**i18n:** Use resolved, user-visible strings in expectations (or a dedicated test locale), not `expect(...).toBe('namespace.key')` on attributes or nodes.

## Runtimes: same contract

- **JSDOM** (e.g. Vitest/Jest + React Testing Library): render UI, query and interact via the same user-facing handles; assert on visible outcomes.
- **Playwright:** same idea in a real browser. Prefer user-visible locators; interactions imply [actionability](https://playwright.dev/docs/actionability) checks. See [Playwright: test user-visible behavior](https://playwright.dev/docs/best-practices#test-user-visible-behavior).

Layout breakage, real focus stacking, and obscured elements are often only honest in **browser-level** tests (or visual regression); JSDOM is not a layout engine.

## Example (aligned interface, different implementation)

```tsx
// Prefer: stable for users, stable for refactors that preserve labels and flow
const name = screen.getByRole('textbox', { name: /organization name/i });
await userEvent.type(name, 'Acme Inc');
await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Continue' }));
expect(screen.getByText(/primary team chat/i)).toBeVisible();
```

Playwright equivalent:

```ts
await page.getByRole('textbox', { name: /organization name/i }).fill('Acme Inc');
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Continue' }).click();
await expect(page.getByText(/primary team chat/i)).toBeVisible();
```

## Heavy setup

If a “small” UI test needs many mocks (routing, auth, flags, stores, i18n, services), the seam is expensive: consider a narrower component under test, a higher-level test with fewer fake layers, or stubs only at real boundaries (network, time, browser APIs).

## Copy changes

If visible copy intentionally changes, **tests should fail** until expectations are updated or the change is reverted. That is the desired signal; do not “fix” tests by re-coupling to implementation to get green.

